## What Are Smart Pointers?

In C++ the language gives you raw pointers (`T*`).  
With raw pointers you are responsible for:

* allocating (`new`)
* de‑allocating (`delete`)
* avoiding double deletes, memory leaks, dangling pointers, etc.

A **smart pointer** is an object that behaves like a pointer (you can dereference it, pass it around, etc.) but it also owns the memory it points to and automatically frees that memory when it is no longer needed.  
All of them are defined in `<memory>` and belong to the `std` namespace:

| Smart pointer | Who owns the memory? | When does the memory get freed? | Typical use case |
|--------------|---------------------|------------------------------|------------------|
| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | **Only one** owner | When the unique_ptr is destroyed or reassigned | “Move‑only” objects that must not be shared |
| `std::shared_ptr<T>` | **Reference‑counted** | When the last shared_ptr is destroyed | Objects that must be shared between several owners |
| `std::weak_ptr<T>` | Does **not** own the memory | Never | Non‑owning, “observer” reference to a shared_ptr object |

All of them follow the **RAII** pattern – the resource is released in the destructor – so you never have to remember to call `delete`.

---

## A Minimal Example

Below is a tiny program that shows all three smart pointers in action.  
The code is intentionally simple; the important parts are the comments.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>      // std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr, std::weak_ptr

// A simple class that prints when it is constructed/destructed.
struct Widget {
    Widget()   { std::cout << "Widget created\n"; }
    ~Widget()  { std::cout << "Widget destroyed\n"; }
    void hello() const { std::cout << "Hello from Widget\n"; }
};

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 1. std::unique_ptr : single owner, no copying                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
void use_unique() {
    // std::make_unique is safer (allocates & constructs in one step)
    std::unique_ptr<Widget> up = std::make_unique<Widget>();
    up->hello();                // access the object
    // when up goes out of scope, Widget is deleted automatically
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 2. std::shared_ptr : reference counting, copyable            */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
void use_shared() {
    std::shared_ptr<Widget> sp1 = std::make_shared<Widget>();
    {
        std::shared_ptr<Widget> sp2 = sp1;   // sp1 and sp2 now share the same Widget
        std::cout << "use_shared: ref count = " << sp1.use_count() << "\n";
        sp2->hello();
    } // sp2 goes out of scope, ref count decremented
    std::cout << "use_shared: ref count after sp2 destroyed = " << sp1.use_count() << "\n";
    // when sp1 goes out of scope, Widget is deleted automatically
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 3. std::weak_ptr : non‑owning observer                      */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
void use_weak() {
    std::shared_ptr<Widget> sp = std::make_shared<Widget>();

    std::weak_ptr<Widget> wp = sp;  // wp does NOT own the Widget
    std::cout << "use_weak: ref count = " << sp.use_count() << "\n";

    // Convert weak_ptr back to shared_ptr (if the object still exists)
    if (auto locked = wp.lock()) {
        locked->hello();            // safe to use
    } else {
        std::cout << "Widget no longer exists\n";
    }

    // Destroy the shared owner
    sp.reset();                     // Widget is deleted

    // Now wp.lock() will return an empty shared_ptr
    if (!wp.lock()) {
        std::cout << "Widget deleted, weak_ptr no longer valid\n";
    }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
int main() {
    std::cout << "=== unique_ptr demo ===\n";
    use_unique();

    std::cout << "\n=== shared_ptr demo ===\n";
    use_shared();

    std::cout << "\n=== weak_ptr demo ===\n";
    use_weak();

    return 0;
}
```

### What Happens When We Run It?

```
=== unique_ptr demo ===
Widget created
Hello from Widget
Widget destroyed

=== shared_ptr demo ===
Widget created
use_shared: ref count = 2
Hello from Widget
use_shared: ref count after sp2 destroyed = 1
Widget destroyed

=== weak_ptr demo ===
Widget created
use_weak: ref count = 1
Hello from Widget
Widget deleted, weak_ptr no longer valid
```

Notice:

* **`unique_ptr`** deletes the object when it leaves scope – you can’t copy it.
* **`shared_ptr`** keeps a reference counter. The object is destroyed when the last shared_ptr is gone.
* **`weak_ptr`** holds a *non‑owning* reference. It can be turned into a `shared_ptr` with `lock()`. If the object has already been destroyed, `lock()` returns an empty `shared_ptr`.

---

## When to Use Which One?

| Scenario | Smart pointer to use | Why |
|----------|----------------------|-----|
| You own an object exclusively and never share it | `std::unique_ptr` | Fastest, no reference counting overhead. |
| You need shared ownership (e.g. multiple parts of a program hold a reference) | `std::shared_ptr` | Automatic lifetime management via reference counting. |
| You need to refer to an object that might outlive the reference but you don't want to keep it alive | `std::weak_ptr` | Prevents a reference cycle that would leak memory. |

**Common patterns**

* **Factory functions**: Return `std::unique_ptr<T>` or `std::shared_ptr<T>` from a factory, letting the caller decide ownership.
* **Callbacks / observers**: Store `std::weak_ptr<T>` in the callback so the observer can be destroyed independently.
* **Threading**: Use `std::shared_ptr` when you hand the same object to several threads.

---

## Quick Checklist

- **Always** prefer smart pointers over raw `new`/`delete`.
- Use `std::make_unique<T>(args...)` or `std::make_shared<T>(args...)` – they are exception‑safe and slightly faster.
- Never mix raw pointers and smart pointers to the same object (unless you’re sure you’re not creating ownership confusion).
- For **exclusive ownership** use `unique_ptr`.  
- For **shared ownership** use `shared_ptr`.  
- For **non‑owning references** use `weak_ptr` (especially to break cycles).

With this foundation you can confidently use smart pointers in any C++ project and avoid the most common pitfalls of manual memory management. Happy coding!
